00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.060
You've probably seen them around. Electric scooters dumped just about anywhere.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:21.100
Litter bikes. Litter bikes. They've been littering the streets lately. And by littering, I mean littering. The scooter invasion. Wheel-mageddon.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.160
They've got the green ones and the orange ones and it's basically litter.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.020
They are bringing out the worst in some people.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:35.600
Last September, the city of Santa Monica,
California woke up to a surprise: sidewalks

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:40.320
everywhere were filled with small, electric
scooters you could rent by the minute.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.789
It’s not an entirely new idea, Scooters,
in some form, have been around forever.

00:00:45.789 --> 00:00:49.900
And you’ve long been able to rent them in
cities like San Fransisco.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.039
But this company, called Bird, made it irresistibly
cheap.

00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:55.090
And simple.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:59.899
All you do is download their app, point your
phone at the scooters’ QR code, I guess

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:04.930
we finally found a use for those, and off
you go at about 15 miles an hour.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:10.070
They’re fast, convenient, if I’m honest,
a little bit ridiculous looking, and… a

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.380
lot of fun.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.520
Unless you’re an investor, In which case,
scooters are no laughing matter.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:23.420
Bird became a unicorn, a startup with a billion
dollar valuation, faster than Uber, Airbnb,

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.500
or Facebook.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.780
Actually, faster than any company in history.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.530
And today, still just over a year since it
was founded, it’s valued at over two billion

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:34.530
dollars,

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.330
about as much as Reddit or 23AndMe.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.820
Remember, we’re talking about scooters.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:46.060
and it’s just one of many, including Spin,
Lime, who is also a unicorn, JUMP, and about

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.260
a dozen others.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:52.910
Meanwhile, Google, Uber, and Lyft have all
invested millions of dollars.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:56.280
Now, depending on who you ask, these are either:

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.140
ridiculous numbers for barely 1-year-old startups
in a fad industry,

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.220
or, smart investments in what’s clearly
the future of transportation.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:07.750
So, which is it?

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:13.440
Are scooters a useful means of mobility or
an invasion of our sidewalks?

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:20.560
Let’s imagine you live in Seattle, say,
an apartment here in the Queen Anne neighborhood.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.210
And you work downtown, here at Amazon corporate
headquarters.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:29.850
You could walk a couple of blocks and take
the bus, but it’ll cost two dollars and

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.790
seventy-five cents, or five fifty a day.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:37.940
And it’ll take at least 20 minutes, which
is just as slow as walking.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:43.500
Biking would be quick and cheap, but then
you have to store, lock, and maintain it.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.910
Plus, no-one wants to arrive at work or school
sweaty.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.590
So, scooters are a nice alternative.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:55.190
It’s faster than walking, Cheaper than the
bus, and more convenient than a bike.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:58.460
Maybe not revolutionary, but pretty handy.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.840
Now, let’s say, you live here, near Lakewood.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.930
In this case, the bus can drop you off right
at work.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:09.840
And the beauty of public transportation is
that it reduces redundancy.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.180
Even if nobody commutes the exact same route,
there’s always going to be a lot of overlap

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.180
in the middle,

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:22.560
If we all share 90% of our journeys, it’s
weird that we take 100% of it in… our own

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:23.760
separate cars.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.900
So, putting people together saves time, space,
and money.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:33.350
Here’s the thing though: Transit is designed
for the average person, but almost no-one

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.870
is exactly the average person.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:41.540
In other words, it’s mostly convenient for
most people, but totally convenient only for

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:42.540
a few.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:47.120
Because, if the bus stopped everywhere, it
would also… stop being useful.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.310
In this example, it’s a 24-minute walk from
home.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.340
This is The First and Last Mile problem,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.780
The hardest and least efficient part of a
trip is the beginning and the end - getting

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.450
to a transit station, and then, to your final
destination.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:05.260
In most cities, the obvious solution is to
walk or bike.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:07.300
But many people just… don’t.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:12.970
It’s too far away, or too inconvenient,
so, they drive instead.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:18.310
That’s probably what you’d end up doing
here, even though transit is technically available.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:24.510
Now, whether scooters are ultimately good
or bad kinda depends on what exactly they’re

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:25.510
replacing.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:30.280
If people scoot instead of walking or biking,
like in the first example, they’ve lost

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:33.470
some exercise and gained some convenience.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:35.800
Not a huge win or loss.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:39.470
But if scooters replace cars, that’s a different
story.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.410
That would mean less traffic congestion and
fewer carbon emissions.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:49.280
Of course, it sounds ridiculous, Even with
their 20 or 30-mile range, they aren’t really

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.190
practical for long trips.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.990
But, they don’t actually have to be.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:55.090
Not directly.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:05:00.300
If scooters make it easier to get to and from
the bus station, you’re more likely to take

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.300
it.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.449
All they need to do is make transit a more
desirable option.

00:05:04.449 --> 00:05:06.940
The effect is fewer cars on the road.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:11.650
That’s especially useful in underserved
and far away neighborhoods.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:16.770
Here, scooters aren’t just a novelty, they’re
a means to greater mobility.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:22.320
Lime showed this in 2017, when it reported
that 40% of riders started or ended their

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.069
bike rides at public transit stations.

00:05:26.069 --> 00:05:29.040
All of this is possible because there’s
always a scooter nearby.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.270
Instead of docks or stations, you pick them
up and leave them wherever.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:37.160
Problem is... well, people pick them up and
leave them wherever.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:42.400
Technically, you’re required to wear a helmet,
park out of people’s way, and not drive

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.610
on the sidewalk.

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:45.860
In practice, ehh, not so much.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:51.790
I’ve yet to see anyone wear a helmet, and
many streets just… don’t have bike lanes.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:55.620
Companies can explain the rules, but they
can’t enforce them.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:57.760
Sooo… cities aren’t the biggest fans.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.180
It doesn’t help that many of these companies
move in to an area before getting permission,

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:07.400
hoping that by the time they notice, people
will have already gotten used to them.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.199
If this sounds familiar, it’s no coincidence.

00:06:10.199 --> 00:06:16.050
Bird’s founder previously worked for Lyft
and Uber, who famously used the same strategy.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.930
This time, cities were ready.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:23.320
They’ve already been banned in San Francisco,
Beverly Hills, Cambridge, and Columbus.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:27.010
And, like Uber, there isn’t much to set
companies apart.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:30.800
They all cost the same dollar to start plus
15 cents a minute,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.669
They even have similar sounding 4-letter names.

00:06:33.669 --> 00:06:35.740
So, which scooter do people choose?

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:37.949
Well, the one that’s in front of them.

00:06:37.949 --> 00:06:41.650
The first app you download will also likely
be your last.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.120
Why bother with several?

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:48.449
That’s why everything is happening so quickly:
they saw what happened with Uber.

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:52.320
This is their second chance, and nobody wants
to be left out.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.910
In China, it happened with bikes.

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:59.850
Companies dumped them on every street and
corner until there was more bike than sidewalk.

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:05.380
Demand just couldn’t keep up with supply,
and now they sit in trash piles so big they’d

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:06.509
impress Wall-E.

00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:10.610
But with enough market share, the economics
are good:

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:16.370
Most companies use the Xiaomi M365, which,
let’s assume they buy in bulk for about

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:18.199
$250.

00:07:18.199 --> 00:07:23.610
Lime says they’re used an average of 8 to
12 times a day, so let’s say, 10 rides,

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.400
at an average of about $3 each.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.590
Of course, there’s also charging.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:33.100
Anyone can sign up to become a charger, or
as Lime calls them, juicers.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:37.560
At night, they pick them up off the streets,
take them home, and plug them in for about

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:38.949
$5-10 a scooter.

00:07:38.949 --> 00:07:41.500
So, we’ll subtract seven fifty.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:45.730
That means the average scooter makes something
like twenty-two fifty a day.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:48.139
And pays for itself in under two weeks.

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:53.320
Even accounting for things like maintenance
and theft, which Lime says affects less than

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.490
1% of its scooters, there’s money to be
made.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:01.460
But what’s most interesting about The Scooter
Wars, may have nothing to do with the scooters

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:02.669
themselves.

00:08:02.669 --> 00:08:06.699
Companies aren’t just competing for space
on the sidewalk, they’re also competing

00:08:06.699 --> 00:08:09.680
for this space - a slot on your home screen.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.530
This is where Uber starts salivating.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:15.410
Anyone with their app can already ride their
scooters.

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:17.880
It’s a built-in advantage.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:21.160
And if you’re already on people’s phones,
why stop there?

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:26.479
There’s no reason to be the taxi company
or the scooter company when you can be, as

00:08:26.479 --> 00:08:30.380
Uber’s new CEO said, “the Amazon of transportation”.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:34.509
Because a smart business sees itself from
the perspective of a customer.

00:08:34.509 --> 00:08:37.570
People think about outcomes, not business
models.

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:41.159
If you want to watch something, you automatically
go to YouTube.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:44.130
If you want to buy something, you go to Amazon.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:48.610
And soon, if you want to go somewhere, you
open Uber.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:53.420
Everything else is unnecessary complexity
companies convince themselves we care about.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:59.420
We’re still in the early days of The Scooter
Wars, but there is good reason to get excited.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:04.570
And the big picture is really about platforms,
the relationship between government and private

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:08.510
corporations, and, increasingly, battery technology.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:14.329
The future of everything from cars, to scooters,
and phones depends on how efficiently we can

00:09:14.329 --> 00:09:15.670
store energy.

